<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="represent-real">
  <title>Representing Real Numbers with Cauchy Sequences of Rational Numbers</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      We say that a Cauchy sequence of rational numbers <m>\{ a_n \}</m> <em>represents</em> the real number <m>r</m> if <m>\displaystyle \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} a_n = r</m>.  For the following problems, do not worry about proofs.  Use these exercises to develop your intuition.  But be able to explain why you make the choices you make.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each of the real numbers <m>47</m> and <m>\frac29</m>, find three Cauchy sequences of rational numbers that represent the number.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>\{ a_n \}</m> is a sequence of rational numbers that represents the real number <m>r</m>, find two other rational sequences that represent <m>r</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each of <m>\pi</m> and <m>e</m>, use your calculator to find the first eight terms of a Cauchy sequence of rational numbers representing the given real number.  Note: If you are willing to look back at your Calculus 2 experience, you may be able to find an entire sequence that represents <m>e</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each real number <m>r</m>, use your experience in #3 to describe how you could construct a Cauchy sequence of rational numbers representing <m>r</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Describe a test for whether or not the rational Cauchy sequences <m>\{ a_n \}</m> and <m>\{ b_n \}</m> represent the same real number.  This test should be understandable to someone who doesn't know what irrational numbers are (but does know all about sequences).
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>